er two turns of such effort or risk fatigue - a fatigued oarsman can only propel the craft at HALF normal speed, and if forced to row at normal speed he becomes exhausted after one turn. A craft can be rowed by fewer characters than the capacity figure, but only at a PROPORTIONATE fraction of its movement rate - the printed example: three people rowing a large rowboat travel 6 miles in eight hours at normal oar (three-quarters of 8) or 45 feet per round at maximum oar (three-quarters of 60). All these rates and modifiers assume at least one character aboard with proficiency in boating. The rules cover only inland-waterway vessels - galleys, merchant ships and warships are out of scope. Census 379 -> 380.);
